          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %em Seifert 3-manifold
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-manifold"} 3-manifold
            which is the total space of a
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+bundle"}> circle bundle
            ) over a 2-dimensional
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold"} orbifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            may not be unique, one also speaks, more explicitly, of
            = succeed "." do
              %em Seifert-fibered manifolds
          %p
            If already the total space is a 3-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold"}> orbifold
            , one correspondingly speaks of a
            %em Seifert orbifold
            etc. (e.g.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#MecchiaSeppi20"} Mecchia &amp; Seppi 2020 Def. 2.6
          %p
            Fully generally one speaks of
            = succeed "." do
              %em Seifert fibrations
          %blockquote
            %p
              (Beware that there is also the
              %em un-related
              notion of
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Seifert+surfaces"} Seifert surfaces
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coboundaries"} coboundaries
              of
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knots"} knots
          %p#LocalStructure
            Accordingly, a general Seifert fibration is locally an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold+quotient"} orbifold quotient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration"} fibration
            of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
